<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Finance</RootNamespace>
		<Description>FIX 4.4 tag-value parsing built with DotGram.</Description>

		<PackageReleaseNotes>The public surface is replaced, and 0.1.0's model is gone from the package: FixParser reads flat typed fields from a string, a byte array, a TextReader or a Stream, and FixMessages builds, validates and walks messages over the fields it is given. Wire and log framing are one enum (FixFraming); the length/data dictionary and the custom pairs are FixFieldOptions; a group comes back as its field sets rather than through typed entry classes, which are withdrawn. A streamed call takes bufferSize and maxRetained, and a field past the limit is an IOException in both the generated and the hand parser. Numbers are decimal, every public member is documented, and a package skill ships beside the README. Two breaks to know about if you are coming from 0.1.0's field reader: FixField.Unknown is now FixField.Custom, so a pattern or a cast naming the old type has to be renamed; and a supplied length/data dictionary now ADDS to the standard's sixteen pairs instead of replacing them, so a dictionary that repeated a standard pair to keep it is now refused with the tag named, and one that meant to add can drop everything it was repeating. FixFieldOptions also takes a FixCustomFields, which builds the fields of tags the package does not define; it builds field objects only, and leaves the tag unknown to the message schema. AND A THIRD BREAK, THE LARGEST: reading the wire and holding it to a schema are now two acts. FixParseMode is gone, and with it the mode parameter of Parse, TryParse, ParseLog and ReadMessages in every form, FixParseOptions(FixParseMode) and FixParseOptions.Mode. The same calls without it build whatever they can read, and message.Validate() answers with everything wrong against the schema — all of it at once, each finding naming its rule, its scope, its tag, the entry of the repeating group it is in, and where in the source it begins. So a message the strict mode used to turn away is now returned and reported on: a required field absent, a required component empty, a value outside its type or code set, a tag twice in one scope, a tag the schema does not place in that scope, a group’s fields out of order, an unknown MsgType, and MessageEncoding absent beside an Encoded field. What still refuses is what the reader cannot defer: framing, BodyLength, CheckSum, a field it could not read, a length/data pair that does not measure, a NumInGroup that sizes an array past the fields left, and a group entry that does not begin with its delimiter. message.Validate(validator) checks against a schema of your own, and DotGram.Finance.Generator compiles a counterparty’s dictionary into one.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>fix;finance;trading;parser;dotgram</PackageTags>

		<!-- The package ships its XML documentation, and every public member has some:
		     under TreatWarningsAsErrors a member left undocumented fails the build. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Handwritten" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44.Tests" />

		<ProjectReference Include="../DotGram/DotGram.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

		<AdditionalFiles Include="Fix/*.gram" />

		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<!-- The skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has restored
		     this package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README
		     says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<None Include="../../LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="licenses/DotGram/" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
